WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:15.519
One of the many delights of using a microscope
is that second before you peer into it, when

00:00:15.519 --> 00:00:23.140
you are only a breath away from seeing into
another world and uncovering organisms familiar

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:25.860
and unfamiliar.

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:30.040
That moment is full of mystery. What will

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:39.170
you find this time…an amoeba under attack?
A tardigrade orgy? Or perhaps an understated,

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:47.540
calming minute of oscillatoria sliding across
the scene? In that moment, anything is possible.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:56.580
But sometimes, there are clues even before
the organisms come into focus, like a whiff

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:00.870
of something a little smelly that alerts you
to synura blooms.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:07.040
Or in the case of Lacrymaria olor, a ciliate
whose name translates to “tears of a swan,”

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:12.000
what you’re looking for is a wanton path
of destruction.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:19.040
In a 1911 paper in the Journal of Animal Behavior,
the scientist S. O. Mast described Lacrymaria

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:24.370
as “among the most interesting of living
beings.”

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:29.940
Now, I’m sure we all have our biases about
what the most interesting living beings actually

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:35.970
are. And on the surface, Lacrymaria don’t
necessarily seem inherently more interesting

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:40.490
than any other organism.
Sure, they’re ciliates, but so are plenty

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:45.903
of other organisms. Also, they get to be around
100 micrometers long,

00:01:45.903 --> 00:01:47.550
they have both macronuclei

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:54.690
and micronuclei, and some contractile vacuoles.
Which again, is all very good, but none of

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:58.600
those details make Lacrymaria particularly
interesting.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:04.360
No, I mean, what we really want to know is
what’s going on with that neck!

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:15.280
The lacrymaria’s teardrop-shaped body extends
off that neck-like thing that has a sort

00:02:15.299 --> 00:02:20.720
of “head” at the end of it. Obviously,
this “head” and “neck” are not actual

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:27.060
heads and necks in our bodily sense of the
words. But the structures are distinct, and

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:30.310
so the terms are convenient ones for us to
use here.

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:37.890
And what makes the Lacrymaria’s neck so
remarkable is just how long it can get. Sure,

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:44.419
the microbe may be named for a swan. But in
his paper, Mast notes the more obvious animal

00:02:44.419 --> 00:02:50.079
comparison, punching the numbers to see how
a Lacrymaria’s neck compares to that of

00:02:50.079 --> 00:02:54.079
a giraffe.
He writes (as dismissively as one can in an

00:02:54.079 --> 00:02:59.930
academic paper) that a giraffe has a neck
that is “scarcely as long as its body.”

00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:08.459
By contrast, the Lacrymaria’s neck can reach
anywhere from 7 to 8 times its body length.

00:03:08.459 --> 00:03:13.650
As Mast goes on to say, if a giraffe could
pull off such a feat, then we would “find

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:20.540
the giraffe browsing leaves from the tops
of trees well towards one hundred feet in

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:27.700
height.” Plus, the Lacrymaria’s neck is
just so flexible, able to wind itself around

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:32.939
debris in a way a giraffe definitely could
not.

00:03:32.939 --> 00:03:38.349
But where giraffes use their relatively shorter
and stiffer necks to forage through treetops,

00:03:38.349 --> 00:03:44.849
Lacrymaria are much less vegan, using their
neck to ambush prey.

00:03:44.849 --> 00:03:55.349
The Lacrymaria’s hunting style is incredibly
successful, but it’s built in part on chaos.

00:03:55.349 --> 00:04:01.730
Instead of lying in wait for food to come
by, the Lacrymaria plays the odds. It zaps

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:09.199
its neck out incredibly fast, sub-second movements
to whip around and randomly sample the area

00:04:09.199 --> 00:04:16.199
around it for food. This strategy helps the
Lacrymaria capture all sorts of prey. And

00:04:16.199 --> 00:04:21.169
that’s why when you’re looking down at
your slides, you might see the damaged remains

00:04:21.169 --> 00:04:26.500
of the Lacrymaria’s unfortunate victims
before you see the hunter itself.

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:35.060
If we saw this kind of carnage on the savannahs
of east africa, pieces of animals strewn across

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:41.110
the landscape, none of us would ever get within
a mile of a giraffe ever again.

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:48.330
When the Lacrymaria finds its target, it fires
off a set of organelles called extrusomes

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:54.450
to release toxins into their prey. And you
can see that the flexibility of the Lacrymaria’s

00:04:54.450 --> 00:05:01.490
neck comes in handy not just for hunting,
but also for eating, expanding as it does

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:04.920
here to contain the entire meal.

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:43.060
And even if the prey is larger or faster than

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:49.689
the Lacrymaria and it can’t get the whole
microbe, it can still tear off quite a sizable

00:05:49.689 --> 00:05:57.099
chunk and make a nice meal out of that piece.
Of course, all that movement is…a lot. And

00:05:57.099 --> 00:06:03.400
it’s not something the organism can be doing
all the time. Our master of microscopes was

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:09.210
excited to find some of these Lacrymaria and
observe them in action, only to find that

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:15.530
they’d become much less active. Their necks
would pull further and further inward until

00:06:15.530 --> 00:06:21.189
it was retracted entirely, and the ciliate
itself was completely still.

00:06:21.189 --> 00:06:27.500
Lacrymaria are a rare enough find as it is,
and when James saw this strange stillness,

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:34.330
he was worried that he might be doing something
that was hurting his precious friends.

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:39.289
Months later, he found some more Lacrymaria
olor. But after 10 minutes of watching them,

00:06:39.289 --> 00:06:45.219
they did the same weird dormant pose thing
again. Concerned again that he might be killing

00:06:45.219 --> 00:06:48.979
them, James left the slide in a humidity chamber
overnight.

00:06:48.979 --> 00:06:55.479
So imagine his relief when the next morning,
he found that the slide was full of reproducing

00:06:55.479 --> 00:06:58.990
Lacrymaria!
That’s when James noticed that this activity

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:05.629
and dormancy seemed to be a repeated cycle.
The Lacrymaria would awake and be active for

00:07:05.629 --> 00:07:10.460
about 10 minutes, and then retreat back to
their inactive state for another 10 minutes,

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:15.819
and then become active again.
Scientists studying this phenomenon have used

00:07:15.819 --> 00:07:22.510
high-speed cameras to find that Lacrymaria
cycle between 4 total states. There’s an

00:07:22.510 --> 00:07:27.460
active state where the microbe’s neck is
out and about to find some food. Then there’s

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:33.340
a resting state where it all retracts. And
then there is an activation and inactivation

00:07:33.340 --> 00:07:38.889
state that transitions the microbe between
active and resting, possibly allowing for

00:07:38.889 --> 00:07:45.580
the structures underlying the neck to rearrange
as the Lacrymaria moves from one way of life

00:07:45.580 --> 00:07:50.090
to the next.
And so while it’s resting, the Lacrymaria

00:07:50.090 --> 00:07:55.860
is much less of a threat. It’s neck, which,
again, is not actually a neck, retracts, leaving

00:07:55.860 --> 00:08:02.150
its neighbors in a moment of peace.
But when it awakens, it will return to twisting

00:08:02.150 --> 00:08:10.790
and turning and elongating, menacing the microcosmos
until it has made a meal of it.

00:08:10.790 --> 00:08:17.520
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

